## 1. vm.c - Original OP_NEGATE Implementation

In the original implementation, OP_NEGATE removes the top value, negates it, and pushes the result back.

```c
case OP_NEGATE:
  push(-pop());
  break;
```

This works correctly, but the stack is decremented and incremented unnecessarily.

---

## 2. vm.c - Optimized OP_NEGATE

I modified OP_NEGATE to negate the top value directly.

```c
case OP_NEGATE:
  vm.stack[vm.stackCount - 1] =
      -vm.stack[vm.stackCount - 1];
  break;
```

This avoids calling pop() and push().

The value changes, but stackCount stays the same.


## 3. vm.c - Original Binary Operation Macro

The original BINARY_OP macro pops two values, performs the arithmetic operation, and pushes the result onto the stack.

```c
#define BINARY_OP(op) \
  do { \
    double b = pop(); \
    double a = pop(); \
    push(a op b); \
  } while (false)
```

The first value popped is the right operand because clox uses a last-in, first-out stack.

---

## 4. vm.c - Optimized Binary Operation Macro

I modified the macro to read both operands directly from the stack and store the result in the left operand's position.

```c
#define BINARY_OP(op) \
  do { \
    double b = vm.stack[vm.stackCount - 1]; \
    double a = vm.stack[vm.stackCount - 2]; \
    vm.stack[vm.stackCount - 2] = a op b; \
    vm.stackCount--; \
  } while (false)
```

Instead of two pops and one push, the optimized version directly updates the stack.

The stack count decreases once because two operands are replaced by one result.

For example:

```text
Before OP_ADD:
Stack: [4, 6]
stackCount: 2

After OP_ADD:
Stack: [10]
stackCount: 1
```

The same optimization works for addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.

---

## 5. vm.c - Arithmetic Instruction Cases

The arithmetic instructions can continue using the BINARY_OP macro.

```c
case OP_ADD:
  BINARY_OP(+);
  break;

case OP_SUBTRACT:
  BINARY_OP(-);
  break;

case OP_MULTIPLY:
  BINARY_OP(*);
  break;

case OP_DIVIDE:
  BINARY_OP(/);
  break;
```

Because BINARY_OP has been optimized, these instructions automatically use the updated implementation.

---

## 6. Performance Comparison

The original OP_NEGATE implementation performs these operations:

```text
1. Pop the top value.
2. Negate the value.
3. Push the result back.
```

The optimized implementation performs:

```text
1. Access the top value.
2. Negate it directly.
```

